      say: `The reads are one task. Listing policies and assignments needs entitlement management read. Group memberships use the transitive call on purpose, because it needs only user read, while another user's direct memberships need directory read. The tools read Graph on the cloud you name. One gap matters here: administrative units with dynamic rules exist only in global Azure, so a GCC High tenant uses the guide's interim path. Nine tests cover this chapter, and all fifty-seven of the kit's tests pass with no tenant.`,
      detail: `The reads are task access-read. List assignment policies: EntitlementManagement.Read.All. List the mover's access package assignments: EntitlementManagement.Read.All. List the mover's group memberships, direct and transitive: User.Read.All (application). The code reads transitiveMemberOf rather than memberOf on purpose: for another user's direct memberships Learn's least-privileged application permission is Directory.Read.All, while the transitive call needs only User.Read.All. The tools read Microsoft Graph on the cloud --cloud names: graph.microsoft.com for GCC, graph.microsoft.us for GCC High, dod-graph.microsoft.us for L5. Learn marks Create assignmentPolicies as available in US Government L4 and L5, and entitlement management, access reviews and dynamic groups as available in Azure Government; GCC uses the commercial service. Administrative units with dynamic membership rules exist only in the global Azure cloud, not in Azure Government, so a GCC High tenant can't scope administration by a rule-based unit; the guide's interim path covers it. The tests: the nine in the Chapter 04 block of tests/test_joiners_and_access.py: the evaluator, the shipped policies, each rule on the broken files, disjoint policies, the scope limit, the export scan, the residual's classes, the completed review's interval and the review sheet. The kit's 57 tests pass under Python 3.14 with no package, tenant or network.`,
